<Fragment slot="heading">What a desktop robot taught me about <span style="font-style:italic">scope</span></Fragment>
<p style="margin:0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">I wanted Stackchan to do everything — talk to me, remind me of things, control other stuff on my desk, maybe read my calendar out loud in the mornings. Within about two weeks I’d built a small creature that did five things badly instead of one thing well.</p>
<p style="margin:22px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">This is an old lesson wearing a new costume. I’ve watched product teams do exactly this with enterprise agents — try to make the first version handle every edge case instead of nailing the core interaction — and I still fell for it on a project with no stakeholders except me and no deadline except my own patience.</p>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600">What I cut</div>
<p style="margin:9px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">The reminders went. The desk-control stuff went. What was left was the one thing that actually made the project interesting in the first place: a consistent, opinionated personality you could have a short conversation with. Everything else was scope creep wearing the disguise of “while I’m at it.”</p>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-family:Newsreader,serif;font-size:25px;line-height:1.25;max-width:52ch">"“While I’m at it” is where most good ideas quietly turn into bad ones."</div>
<p style="margin:34px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">Cutting scope on a side project with no one watching is a strange kind of practice. There’s no roadmap pressure forcing the discipline — you have to supply it yourself, which turns out to be the actual skill.</p>
<div style="margin-top:26px;border:1px dashed var(--dash)"><div data-shot="1" style="height:190px;background:var(--stripe);display:flex;align-items:flex-end;padding:20px"><span style="font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;font-size:10.5px;color:var(--muted);letter-spacing:.08em">[ OPTIONAL DIAGRAM ]</span></div></div>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600">The nugget</div>
<p style="margin:9px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">If you can’t describe what your thing does in one sentence without an “and,” it isn’t ready to be judged yet. Cut until it is.</p>
</BlogPost>
